    "evidence_text": "Most microRNAs were underexpressed in older individuals.\n\nAdditionally, \u0394age was associated with coronary heart disease, hypertension, blood pressure, and glucose levels.\n\nBACKGROUND Age-associated epigenetic changes are implicated in aging.\n\nCONCLUSIONS Age-associated hypo- and hypermethylation events occur at distinct regulatory features of the genome.\n\nDNA methylation age of human tissues and cell types\n\n DNA methylation and healthy human aging\n\nWidespread and tissue specific age-related DNA methylation changes in mice.\n\nWe then examined 11 changed genomic loci for age-related methylation in other tissues.\n\nRecent evidence demonstrates a role for paternal aging on offspring disease susceptibility.\n\nSurvival was evaluated using Cox proportional hazards regression models.   \n\n\nIncreasing vascular inflammation, as measured by CRP, increases the likelihood of death.\n\nCaloric restriction delays age-related methylation drift\n\nIn mammals, caloric restriction consistently results in extended lifespan.\n\nWe propose that epigenetic drift is a determinant of lifespan in mammals.\n\nCaloric restriction has been shown to increase lifespan in mammals.\n\nRapid DNA methylation changes after exposure to traffic particles.\n\nNo association was found with Alu methylation (P > 0.12).   \n\n\nWhether decreased methylation mediates exposure-related health effects remains to be determined.",
